# 🔐 Enterprise SSH Access & Key Control Center (SSH Manager)

An enterprise-grade, web-based **SSH Access & Public Key Lifecycle Management System** powered by **Flask (Python)**, **MySQL 8.0**, and **Ansible Core**. 

Designed to automate SSH key deployment, enforce Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), audit authorized key files across multi-server Linux fleets, and execute automated time-bound key expirations.

---

## 🏗️ System Architecture

SSH Manager uses a **decoupled 4-tier architecture** separating presentation, business logic, persistence, and infrastructure execution.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph Client Tier [Frontend Presentation Layer]
        UI[Glassmorphic SPA - HTML5 / CSS3 / Vanilla JS]
    end

    subgraph App Tier [Backend Service & Logic Layer]
        Flask[Flask REST API Server - Python 3.10+]
        AuthRBAC[Session Auth & RBAC Middleware]
        Scheduler[Daemon Expiration Scheduler Thread]
        AnsibleRunner[Ansible Subprocess Execution Wrapper]
    end

    subgraph Data Tier [Persistence Layer - MySQL 8.0]
        MySQL[(MySQL Database: Ansible)]
        AC[access_cache]
        SKC[ssh_key_cache]
        JH[job_history]
        TK[active_temp_keys]
        RBAC[app_users / roles / permissions]
        SM[server_metadata]
        SCL[sync_change_log]
    end

    subgraph Infra Tier [Managed Linux Fleet]
        AnsibleEngine[Ansible Core Engine]
        Serv1[Target Server 1: serv2]
        Serv2[Target Server 2: serv3]
        ServN[Target Server N: node...]
    end

    UI -->|REST API Requests / JSON| Flask
    Flask --> AuthRBAC
    Flask --> Scheduler
    Flask -->|PyMySQL Connection| MySQL
    
    MySQL --- AC
    MySQL --- SKC
    MySQL --- JH
    MySQL --- TK
    MySQL --- RBAC
    MySQL --- SM
    MySQL --- SCL

    Flask --> AnsibleRunner
    AnsibleRunner -->|Playbook Invocation| AnsibleEngine
    AnsibleEngine -->|SSH Port 22 / Single-Pass fetch_keys.yml| Serv1
    AnsibleEngine -->|SSH Port 22 / manage_keys.yml| Serv2
    AnsibleEngine -->|SSH Port 22| ServN
```

### Architectural Layer Breakdown

1. **Presentation Layer (Frontend)**: Single Page Application (SPA) built with Vanilla JS and a modern Glassmorphism UX design system. Renders real-time dashboards for Global Key Search, Access Matrix, Server Explorer, Key Sharing Wizard, and Audit Logs.
2. **Application & API Layer (Backend)**: Built with Flask. Exposes REST endpoints, enforces session security, masks sensitive keys for non-admin accounts (`mask_ssh_key()`), wraps Ansible execution via Python `subprocess.Popen`, and runs a 30-second background thread worker (`check_temp_key_expirations`) for automated time-based access revocations.
3. **Persistence Layer (MySQL)**: B-Tree indexed MySQL database acting as the single source of truth for all SSH keys, user home directories, server metadata, and job execution logs. Decouples fast UI searches (<1ms responses) from slow network SSH calls.
4. **Infrastructure Layer (Ansible)**: Executes key discovery and deployment directly on target Linux nodes using SSH (Port 22) and Ansible Core playbooks.

---

## 🗄️ Database Architecture & ER Schema

The database consists of **10 specialized MySQL tables** managing host access states, SSH key attributes, audit logs, and dashboard RBAC.

```mermaid
erDiagram
    app_users ||--o{ roles : "belongs to"
    roles ||--o{ role_permissions : "has"
    permissions ||--o{ role_permissions : "granted to"

    server_metadata ||--o{ access_cache : "tracks"
    access_cache ||--o{ ssh_key_cache : "contains"
    job_history ||--o{ active_temp_keys : "records"
    server_metadata ||--o{ sync_change_log : "audits"

    app_users {
        bigint id PK
        string username UK
        string password_hash
        string full_name
        string email
        int role_id FK
        boolean is_active
        datetime last_login_at
    }

    roles {
        int id PK
        string role_name UK
        string description
    }

    permissions {
        int id PK
        string perm_key UK
        string description
    }

    access_cache {
        bigint id PK
        string host UK
        string user UK
        int keys_count
        boolean has_access
        string status
        mediumtext keys_text
        datetime updated_at
    }

    ssh_key_cache {
        bigint id PK
        string host UK
        string user UK
        string fingerprint UK
        string home_dir
        string algorithm
        text key_body
        text raw_key
        string comment
        boolean is_duplicate
        datetime last_synced_at
    }

    job_history {
        string id PK
        datetime timestamp
        string action
        string target_user
        text target_hosts
        string status
        longtext logs
        double duration
        string operator_name
        string expires_at
    }

    active_temp_keys {
        string id PK
        string host
        string user
        text ssh_key
        string fingerprint
        string expires_at
        boolean revoked
    }

    server_metadata {
        string host PK
        string ip
        string group_name
        string environment
        string region
        string status
        datetime last_ping_at
    }

    sync_change_log {
        int id PK
        string job_id
        string operator_name
        string host
        string user
        string change_type
        text description
    }
```

### Table Descriptions

* `access_cache`: Aggregated server-user access states (`active`, `disabled`, `none`) and raw `authorized_keys` text.
* `ssh_key_cache`: Parsed SSH public keys indexed by algorithm (`rsa`, `ed25519`, `ecdsa`), SHA256 fingerprint, body, comment, and duplicate status across servers.
* `job_history`: Complete audit trail of Ansible execution jobs, command outputs, operator identities, and run durations.
* `active_temp_keys`: Active temporary access provisions, target expiration timestamps, and revocation state.
* `server_metadata`: Managed host inventory details, IP addresses, Ansible groups, regions, and environment tags.
* `sync_change_log`: Granular audit log capturing login events, user creation, server edits, and key changes.
* `app_users`, `roles`, `permissions`, `role_permissions`: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) mapping system roles (`admin`, `developer`) to granular permissions (`read:keys`, `write:keys`, `view:full_key`, `manage:servers`, `manage:users`, `trigger:sync`).

